<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ludovico Sforza</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1452–1508</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Ludovico il Moro · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-sforza">House of Sforza</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ludovico Sforza, called il Moro, ruled the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> as regent from 1480 and as duke from 1494 to 1499. A great patron of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>, he employed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonardo-da-vinci">Leonardo da Vinci</a>, who painted the Last Supper and planned an equestrian monument for him. His encouragement of the French invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 1494 backfired: Louis XII took Milan in 1499, and Ludovico died a prisoner in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ludovico-sforza.jpg" alt="Ludovico Sforza" width="320" height="385" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown Master, Italian (active 1490-1520 in Lombardy) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pala_Sforzesca_-_detail_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ludovico Maria Sforza was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> on 3 August 1452, the fourth son of Francesco I Sforza and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bianca-maria-visconti">Bianca Maria Visconti</a>. As a fourth son he was not expected to rule, but his mother still wanted him well educated. His teachers included the humanist Francesco Filelfo, and he studied Greek, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, theology, painting, sculpture and government.</p><p>His brother Duke Galeazzo Maria was assassinated on 26 December 1476, and was succeeded by his seven-year-old son Gian Galeazzo Maria. Ludovico opposed the regency of the duchess Bona and her councillor Cicco Simonetta. The attempt failed, and Ludovico was exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a>. He later took control of the city and sought alliances with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorenzo-de-medici">Lorenzo de' Medici</a>, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-i-of-naples">Ferrante of Naples</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-sixtus-iv">Pope Sixtus IV</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Ludovico had been betrothed to Beatrice d'Este, daughter of Ercole d'Este, when she was five. He postponed the wedding three times, having met Cecilia Gallerani, and decided to marry only after his nephew's marriage, consummated in 1490, produced the prospect of an heir. Gian Galeazzo and his wife Isabella of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-aragon">Aragon</a> left for Pavia, and Gian Galeazzo was content to leave his uncle in charge. Beatrice bore a son, Ercole Massimiliano, in 1493 and quarrelled with Isabella over who should hold the title of Count of Pavia.</p><p>Gian Galeazzo died on 22 October 1494 in mysterious circumstances. Officially his death was ascribed to a long illness and an immoderate life, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niccolo-machiavelli">Niccolò Machiavelli</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francesco-guicciardini">Francesco Guicciardini</a> believed Ludovico had poisoned him, which the historian Malaguzzi Valeri disputes. Ludovico succeeded with the support of the Milanese nobles. Threatened by King Alfonso II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, he had called the French into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. When the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a> occupied Novara and Vigevano, he considered fleeing to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, but his wife and some councillors persuaded him to stay. Beatrice died in childbirth on the night of 2 January 1497, and Ludovico never recovered from his grief.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a> succeeded Charles VIII as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xii-of-france">Louis XII of France</a> in 1498 and launched a second expedition against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, under the command of Gian Giacomo Trivulzio, a personal enemy of Ludovico. Ludovico fled and was taken prisoner to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. He was held in several places and from 1504 at the castle of Loches. After an attempt to escape in 1508 he was deprived of his books and other comforts, and he died in the dungeon on 27 May 1508.</p><p>Contemporaries called him mild and generous, and a patron of artists and writers whose court became one of the most important in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> during the Italian Renaissance. Guicciardini named him the arbiter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. His nickname il Moro, the Moor, was given when he was a child, and is thought to come from his dark complexion, hair and eyes.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ludovico Sforza” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The French invasions changed everything: Louis XII, claiming the duchy through his grandmother Valentina Visconti, conquered it in 1499, and <b>Ludovico Sforza</b> was captured at Novara in 1500.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-milan">Duchy of Milan · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Vigevano, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Loches, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.32,8.87&amp;year=1488" aria-label="Vigevano on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4761 565 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ludovico Sforza"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5211 835h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5211 835h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.32,8.87&amp;year=1488">What was here in 1488? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
